WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.180
It's a new day on Tesla's short-time, daily, episode 499,

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:05.980
coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.860
It's June 3, 2026, and the pace of updates

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.760
around Tesla's vehicles and software

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.020
keeps layering one practical change on top of another.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.380
Rather than running through a checklist,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.600
let's walk through how these pieces connect

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.020
for owners who live with the cars every day.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.720
One of the more visible refinements

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.240
shows up inside the cabin.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.820
Tesla's interior accent lights now shift to red

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.880
when another vehicle sits in a blind spot,

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.600
giving drivers an immediate visual signal

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.120
without needing to check the mirrors.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.160
The system separates left side and right side detection

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.440
and widens its coverage at intersections,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.300
all while using the existing individual LED controls

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.160
already in the doors and panels.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.380
What this means in practice is that a driver

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.780
can keep attention forward during lane changes

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.200
or merges and still catch the cue in peripheral vision.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.380
It also removes the need for extra hardware,

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:02.720
so the feature rolls out through software

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.260
rather than a service visit.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.620
That same approach shows up again

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.280
when we look at how Tesla handles driver attention

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:10.540
inside the car.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.500
The cabin camera's eye-tracking logic

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.340
has been tightened in the version 14 series

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.140
so that full self-driving issues fewer unnecessary prompts.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.440
Instead of nagging at every brief glance away,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.200
the system now builds a more nuanced picture

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.060
of whether the driver is actually engaged with the road.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.220
The intent is to cut down on interruptions

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.620
while the core safety requirement stays in place.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.480
For anyone who has felt the earlier versions

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.080
were overly sensitive on long highway stretches,

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.740
this adjustment should feel like a direct response

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.220
to real usage patterns.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.960
It also sets up the next regulatory conversations,

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.120
because any change to how attentiveness is measured

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.000
will likely be part of upcoming reviews of supervised systems.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.300
Those regulatory questions are already surfacing

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.680
in another corner of the FSD story.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.260
Tesla quietly updated older, full self-driving

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.380
purchase agreements by inserting the word

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.220
supervised into the language.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.360
Owners discovered the change after the fact

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.420
with no fresh signatures or advanced notice.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.700
The edit simply brings the contracts into line

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.320
with how regulators currently describe the feature,

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.700
but the retroactive nature raises the possibility

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.040
that some owners may push back and trigger legal discussions.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.260
In the meantime, the company is moving ahead

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:33.100
on the practical side by expanding where owners

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.240
can actually use the supervised system

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:36.640
with insurance in place.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.220
Lemonade has introduced a new policy line in Indiana

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.240
aimed specifically at Tesla owners running supervised FSD.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.280
The coverage factors in the shift in liability

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:51.040
that can occur when the driver is still ultimately responsible

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.940
yet the car is handling more of the routine work.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:56.960
This is one of the first usage-based products

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.140
tied directly to Tesla's hardware and software stack,

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.360
and it will be worth watching to see whether similar offerings

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:04.980
appear in other states soon.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.320
Insurance access matters because it removes one friction point

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.320
for drivers who want to test longer supervised routes

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.020
without coverage gaps.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.200
Real-world testing on those longer routes

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.680
is already producing data points worth noting.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.460
A model Y recently covered 150 miles

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.720
on the Pennsylvania Turnpike under version 14.3.3,

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.320
and the results were mixed.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.020
Clean highway segments ran smoothly with minimal input,

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:36.260
yet construction zones and certain edge situations

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:38.280
still required the driver to step in.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.660
The report highlighted strong straightaway performance

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.860
alongside those occasional interventions,

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.580
which suggests the current software handles

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.400
predictable high-speed travel better than it handles

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.640
variable or temporary road conditions.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.820
More long distance runs will be needed before anyone can say

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:58.660
how close the system is to handling the full range

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.000
of highway variability without assistance.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.340
While the testing continues, another small

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.540
but useful cabin change has arrived.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.220
The accent lighting that now doubles

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.920
as a blind spot indicator uses separate left

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.600
and right detection plus wider angle sensing at intersections.

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:19.140
Because it works through the existing LED array,

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.420
there is no added cost or hardware install for owners.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:25.540
The practical effect is that drivers receive

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:27.680
an extra layer of situational awareness

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.260
that integrates with the same lighting already present

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:31.240
for ambient use.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.720
It sits alongside the improved eye tracking logic

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.860
as another incremental step that reduces the mental load

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.620
without changing the fundamental supervised nature

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.420
of the system.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.260
Owners are also seeing more value

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.240
from the cameras that support those features.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.400
Dash cam and sentry mode now capture a full 24 hours

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.460
of rolling footage instead of the earlier one hour buffer.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.120
The software change simply extends

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.160
how the onboard memory is managed,

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.960
so no new storage hardware is required.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:04.500
In daily use, this means an owner can scroll back

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.640
through an entire day's worth of activity

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:08.040
when reviewing an incident,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.460
rather than stitching together short clips.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.800
That extended window becomes especially relevant

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.240
for insurance claims or security reviews

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.940
where context across several hours can matter.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.980
It also underscores how Tesla's control

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.140
over both the vehicle operating system

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:26.420
and the camera hardware lets it deliver

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:28.600
these increments through over the air updates,

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.740
something traditional automakers

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:34.220
with multiple supplier layers rarely match at the same pace.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.620
Storage improvements help with day-to-day ownership

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:41.200
yet longer-term questions around value retention remain.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:45.560
A recent depreciation analysis found that the model Y

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.100
holds its value more steadily

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.340
than some comparable luxury SUVs in the first year,

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.620
then experiences a steeper drop afterward.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.120
Battery health and the ability to retain

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:59.060
the latest software updates are cited as key factors

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:00.320
in the model outcomes.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.500
Because Tesla sells directly and continues

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.360
to push updates to older vehicles,

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.640
those elements could shape how they used market prices

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:09.960
these cars going forward.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:12.840
That resale picture becomes more relevant

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:14.940
as Tesla prepares wider deliveries

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:16.720
of its lower priced cyber truck.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:21.600
The $59,990 base cyber truck units

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.880
have begun arriving at US service centers.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.860
This configuration removes the permanent

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.500
full-self driving hardware from the included price,

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:31.400
which lowers the entry point

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:33.920
while keeping the option available for later purchase.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.580
Logistics are scaling ahead of customer handovers

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.120
and the volume of deliveries in the coming weeks

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.180
will indicate whether the reduced price

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.280
actually broadens the buyer pool

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:47.060
beyond the earlier higher cost variance.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.940
Taken together, these updates show Tesla continuing

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.600
to refine both the visible

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.060
and the behind-the-scenes elements of the vehicle.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.600
The lighting change and the extended recording buffer

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.760
both rely on software scheduling

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.440
of existing components rather than new parts.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:07.340
The insurance product and the contract language adjustment

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.580
both reflect the ongoing effort

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:14.440
to align supervised FSD with regulatory and liability realities.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:18.140
The eye tracking refinement and the highway test results

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:20.120
both point to incremental progress

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.460
that still leaves room for further work on edge cases

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:27.760
and the depreciation data alongside the new cyber truck pricing.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.540
Both speak to how buyers will weigh long-term ownership costs

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:33.340
as more vehicles reach the market.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.320
Keep an eye on whether the Indiana insurance approach spreads

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.040
and whether the longer dash cam buffer leads

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:42.540
to similar extensions for other logging features.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:45.240
The next indicator will likely come

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.360
from how these supervised systems perform

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:49.600
across additional long-distance routes

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:51.140
and how regulators respond

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.200
to the updated monitoring logic.

